{:edb/db-before-t 2015 :edb/db-after-t 2016 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535328 50 #inst "2026-10-08T22:13:00.765Z" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119535 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062864/assets/9129b6fbd360b9b9bcc937fd2acf0aedc25681d4b3dca100be7f681b7bb347dd.png) \n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{42} \\cdot u_{24}?$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119535 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{42} \\cdot u_{24}$?" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119535 1145 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U$, as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 1R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & R_{4}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{4} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{4} \\mid:=R_{4} + (-2)R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, since $l_{42}=-2$ and $u_{24}=-3$, we get\n\n$$\nl_{42} \\cdot u_{24} = (-2) \\cdot (-3) =6\n$$" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119535 1145 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & \\,2\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062864/assets/ac086686e36fdc3e3fffbf4e772f5bdd3d797a71cd06a8e0c4c0bc338051371e.png)\n\nFinally, since $l_{42}=-2$ and $u_{24}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{42} \\cdot u_{24} = (-2) \\cdot (-3) =6.\n$$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119536 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062864/assets/092a8a3d8ca1efa88189085838d4782072a254ac3790cf01f25ad9b5d0bca0ca.png)\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry $l_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119536 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry $l_{31}$ in the matrix $L$?" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119536 1145 "We want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row of $A$, and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row of $A$ multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-4}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry $l_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to $R_1$, taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\nl_{31}=-({\\color{purple}-4})=4\n$$" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119536 1145 "We want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row of $A,$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row of $A$ multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-4}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry $l_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to $R_1,$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\nl_{31}=-({\\color{purple}-4})=4\n$$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119628 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062864/assets/8b39a1d4b72f3ab3bfc6758a73e5c1729d9261be539777b45959c342d4bda4da.png)\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119628 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U$?" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119628 1145 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-6)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -26\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u_{33}=6$." 13194139535328 false] [17592186119628 1145 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u_{33}=6.$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119629 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062864/assets/e819307ecef66a628ff3b0b03d98c6e0f054bbb6c78cd9e7395b99e45f54000a.png)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{31} \\cdot u_{23}?$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119629 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{31} \\cdot u_{23}$?" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119629 1145 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U$, as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 24 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, since $l_{31}=-2$ and $u_{23}=-9$, we get\n\n$$\nl_{31} \\cdot u_{23} = (-2) \\cdot (-9) =18\n$$" 13194139535328 false] [17592186119629 1145 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 24 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & \\,1\\, & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062864/assets/1ae6992336eda022f30a643ff0e89ae46c37978ac193370e518ecbea5084878b.png)\n\nFinally, since $l_{31}=-2$ and $u_{23}=-9,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{31} \\cdot u_{23} = (-2) \\cdot (-9) =18.\n$$" 13194139535328 true] [17592186119721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343958 13194139535328 true] [17592186119721 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343965 13194139535328 true] [17592186119721 1020 #edb/ref 1